<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert Annotation.</span>
All translations were evaluated by the same three domain experts as in Section <a href="#S2.SS1.SSS2" title="2.1.2 Dataset Construction ‣ 2.1 DITING ‣ 2 Method ‣ DITING: A Multi-Agent Evaluation Framework for Benchmarking Web Novel Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1.2</span></a> under a rigorously defined annotation protocol (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Annotation Details ‣ DITING: A Multi-Agent Evaluation Framework for Benchmarking Web Novel Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).
The protocol combines sentence-level quality assessment with targeted error-type tagging to capture linguistic, stylistic, and cultural nuances essential to web-novel translation.
Annotators assessed each output along six dimensions (Table <a href="#S2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 2.2 MetricAlign ‣ 2 Method ‣ DITING: A Multi-Agent Evaluation Framework for Benchmarking Web Novel Translation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), assigning discrete scores (2 / 1 / 0) and providing brief justifications for borderline cases. Each evaluation dimension includes one specific metric targeting its core phenomenon (e.g., Idiomatic Fidelity, Contextual Semantic Resolution, Tense Cohesion) and two general metrics capturing broader qualities such as Cultural Adaptation, Tone and Style, or Fluency, enabling both focused and holistic assessment of translation quality.</p>
</div>
